# Line endings are LF in the working tree on EVERY platform.
#
# Why this file exists: this repo is developed on Windows and Linux and built on
# both. With `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git-for-Windows default) a Windows
# checkout rewrites every text file to CRLF while the repository keeps LF, so the
# same commit produces different bytes on different machines. That difference is
# invisible in `git diff` and shows up later as:
#
#   * source-level contract tests failing ONLY on Windows, because they assert a
#     literal containing "\n" against a file that is physically CRLF (this cost a
#     real debugging session on 2026-08-17 — three failures in
#     desktop-publication-contract.test.ts that were pure checkout artifacts);
#   * shell scripts carrying a trailing CR into commands, paths and heredocs,
#     where the failure mode is an unreadable `$'\r': command not found`;
#   * checksum drift, because a file's sha256 depends on its line endings and the
#     desktop release pipeline pins font and artifact digests.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf` per repository, so nobody has to configure
# their machine correctly for a checkout to be correct.
#
# Applying this to an existing checkout is a one-time, content-neutral step, run
# on a CLEAN tree:  git add --renormalize .
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only artifacts that genuinely need CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.pbf  binary
*.wasm binary
*.pmtiles binary
*.glb  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.sqlite binary
